# decision Manifest-changing commands are transactions

`add`, `update`, `set`, `remove`, and `wire` stage every manifest, lock, cache, surface, vendored
tree, and ecosystem
edit before publishing any of them. A failure before commit leaves every tracked file byte-for-byte
unchanged; a commit failure rolls back already-published files. `wire` is idempotent and may repair
an intentionally partial dependency without changing its identity.

# decision Exit codes classify outcomes

Exit 0 means the requested state was reached (warnings may still be present). Exit 1 means a valid
request found drift, failed trust, a missing Refresh prerequisite, or another operational failure.
Exit 2 means invocation or resolution failed: invalid flags, absent input, unknown identity, or a
location from which no input could be read. Preserve these classes across subcommands.

# decision Output separates records from diagnostics

Machine-consumable records and command results go to stdout, one record per line unless the command
explicitly emits JSON or a document. Progress, warnings, summaries, and errors go to stderr. Durable
state belongs in the manifest, lock, or `.git-a2a/`; command delivery history is never invented.

# invariant Dry runs and checks do not mutate

`--dry-run`, `--check`, offline inspection, help, and validation paths do not change tracked files,
the lock, or `.git-a2a/`.

# decision Dependency status and roster refresh are distinct

The status table contains dependencies only; the consumer module is reported once in a footer,
with detailed diagnostics reserved for `-v`. An absent managed `AGENTS.md` block is `SYNC none`
and healthy, while an existing block that differs from the renderer is `stale`. After `sync` has
created a block, every dependency mutation re-renders that existing block as its final step and
never creates one implicitly. `--no-refresh` on add, update, set, pin, unpin, and wire skips the
package-manager Refresh phase without skipping deterministic manifest, lock, wiring, or roster
updates. Human status output expands its table with spaces through Go's tabwriter so every column
starts at the same position even when a source, ref, or wiring summary is longer than the header.
Human output includes the ASCII-only `VENDOR` column only when at least one manifest or lock
dependency is vendored; ordinary native-Git consumers keep the compact pre-vendoring table.
When present its values are `none`, `submodule @<sha7>`, `copy`, `missing`, or `drift`; ASCII
keeps tabwriter byte width equal to terminal cell width. JSON always includes `vendor` so its
machine shape never depends on repository state.

# decision Prerequisite reporting is shared with adapters

`doctor` always checks Git >= 2.25, warns below 2.27, and unions tools from detected ecosystem
markers with ecosystems explicitly named by wired dependencies. Human and JSON output include
version, PATH state, and the platform install hint; any required missing or incompatible tool is
exit 1. `status -v` exposes the same state. `add`, `wire`, `update`, and `set` preserve a completed
manifest edit and warn when only downstream Refresh is unavailable; a direct adapter `Refresh`
returns the shared actionable error.
When multiple build systems share one export ecosystem, prerequisite lookup continues past
non-matching detectors and reports the tool for the detected implementation (for example Gradle
for a `maven` export in a repository with `settings.gradle.kts`).

# decision Fetch restores cache from immutable lock state

`git-a2a fetch [ID ...]` is the durable-state repair command for reconstructible local state.
It fetches each manifest at the exact lock commit, validates the manifest SHA-256, stages the
cache, and atomically replaces only that dependency's cache. `--surface` also verifies the locked
tree id. For a declared vendor it restores the submodule or copy at the exact lock identity without
changing the lock or adapter files. It never resolves the manifest ref, rewrites manifest/lock,
snapshots cards, or invokes adapters; missing lock coordinates or hash mismatches exit 1.

# decision Vendoring participates in every dependency lifecycle

`add --vendor submodule|copy [--vendor-path P]` makes the consumer choice explicit. `set` changes
mode/path or `--no-vendor`; `update` moves the materialisation with the lock; `fetch` and `wire`
restore missing materialisation; `remove` removes all Git residue. `set`, `update`, and `remove`
refuse dirty/drifted vendor state unless `--force` makes data loss explicit. Transport rollback
restores the old materialisation when later metadata, cache, or adapter publication fails.
`set` materialises the candidate vendor before adapter Refresh so file/path resolvers can read
it; the transport rollback remains live until every later publication step succeeds.

# decision Usage is a deterministic agent briefing

`git-a2a usage` prints at most 60 lines with exactly eight routine commands, one example each,
exit classes, structured-output guidance, and the canonical reference. `--prompt` appends the
complete fresh-agent workflow and `--json` returns the same ordered lines. Both forms are embedded
constants: they do not inspect the repository, network, terminal, clock, or environment.

# decision Authoring helpers construct valid manifests without prompts

`agent add/remove/list`, `export add`, and `policy set` are non-interactive, noun-first helpers
for manifest authors. Mutations load and validate the existing manifest, edit only the owned
`yaml.Node` sequence or mapping entries (preserving comments, key order, extensions, and
flow/block style of untouched nodes), validate again, emit YAML atomically, and refresh an existing
AGENTS.md managed block. A roster-refresh failure restores the original manifest and roster.
Repeated `--scope` and `--contact` flags preserve declaration order; contact fields use
comma-separated `key=value` pairs and `|` only inside list values such as intents and labels.
They accept `--yes` as a no-op for automation. `init --example lib|app` writes a complete valid,
commented starting manifest and never overwrites an existing one. `policy set --may`,
`--may-not`, and `--notes` replace only the explicitly supplied consumer-policy fields.

# decision Explain embeds the generated normative field reference

`git-a2a explain PATH [--json]` reads no repository, environment, clock, or network. Its content
is the byte-identical generated `docs/manifest-reference.md` embedded at build time; the docs
generator updates and drift-checks both copies, so the command cannot become a second normative
source. Authors may omit `[]` from array paths (`agents.contacts.kind` resolves to
`agents[].contacts[].kind`). Unknown paths exit 2.

# decision Setup installs repository-scoped agent guidance only

`git-a2a setup` detects Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, GitHub Copilot, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Hermes
Agent, and OpenClaw from repository markers. User-home markers are reported with an explicit
`--harness <name>` instruction and never cause repository writes. `--harness a,b` selects named
harnesses and `--all` selects every integration. It always installs a thin cross-agent skill
(`SKILL.md` plus `references/README.md`) at `.agents/skills/git-a2a/`, mirrors it to `.claude/skills/`
when Claude Code is present, ensures a separately delimited AGENTS.md pointer, and adds the
project-scoped `git-a2a mcp` entry supported by each detected harness. Unrelated configuration
and guidance survive. `--dry-run` and `--check` are non-mutating; check exits 1 on drift. Setup
never downloads or installs the executable, a harness, a toolchain, or any dependency.
Hermes Agent and OpenClaw expose only user-scoped MCP registries; setup must not mutate those
home-directory files. When detected it prints their exact `hermes mcp add` / `openclaw mcp set`
command instead, leaving the operator to authorize that wider scope.

# decision CLI help and automation are uniform

Every top-level and nested command help puts a runnable `Examples:` section immediately after usage and ends
with the shared `Exit codes:` legend. `--timeout` and no-op `--yes` are accepted globally, so an
agent can pass them to any command without discovering command-specific exceptions. Commands do
not prompt. Read commands `who`, `show`, `status`, `doctor`, `validate`, `explain`, and `usage`
offer JSON; output written to a non-TTY contains no ANSI escapes.

# decision MCP is a thin command-scoped projection of the CLI

`git-a2a mcp` is a stateless stdio server built on the official Go SDK v1.7.x. It opens no
listener and exits with its harness connection. Seven read tools and the cache-restoring `fetch`
tool delegate to the existing JSON CLI paths; six write-capable tools are absent unless the operator starts it with
`--allow-write`. `remove` remains CLI-only. The fixed resources expose the current manifest,
lock, freshly rendered roster, and embedded generated field reference. Tool schemas are typed,
tool order is golden-tested, and every tool plus the spawned stdio transport has a protocol
round-trip test. `setup` writes the default read-only command for every harness, including
Claude Code's project `.mcp.json`; it never silently grants write access.
Every repository-dependent tool accepts `root`, resolved relative to the server startup root when
not absolute. An omitted root preserves the startup repository. Multiple stdio instances share no
listener or mutable server state and may run concurrently; one instance may also switch roots per
tool call. Fixed resources remain bound to the startup root so their stable URIs do not change
meaning mid-session.
`fetch` is annotated non-read-only, non-destructive, idempotent, and open-world because it
recreates disposable cache through Git access. `contact` is non-idempotent because every call
delivers. A tool `root` may reach any path allowed by the process; with `--allow-write` the host
is the trust boundary. A `--roots` allow-list is explicitly deferred.
Write-enabled MCP `add` and `set` expose the same `vendor`, `vendorPath`, `noVendor`, and `force`
choices as the CLI and delegate them as ordinary command flags; MCP does not invent a separate
materialisation model.
